call_details.response:
The current position has no immediate winning move for o (player to move). However, x has two distinct ways to create an immediate threat on the subsequent turn: dropping in column 1 (creating a horizontal threat on the bottom row) or dropping in column 2 (creating a vertical threat).  

Column 2 is the unique move that simultaneously  
- neutralizes x’s most advanced vertical threat, and  
- places an o-piece that forms the solid diagonal three-in-a-row (3,2)–(4,3)–(5,4).  

After this drop the forced continuation is:

1. x must create a threat of his own by playing column 1;  
2. o’s answering drop in column 0 not only blocks that threat but also completes a gapped four-in-a-row whose only empty cell (4,1) is immediately playable, thereby returning a new threat;  
3. x is forced to block at (4,1);  
4. o’s next drop in column 1 activates the original diagonal (the cell (2,1) becomes playable), returning yet another threat;  
5. x is forced to block again.  

At the end of this exchange it is again o’s turn and o possesses the initiative together with several intact building pairs. Every alternative first move leaves x at least one intact threat-creation option, after which o is forced to answer without being able to generate a counter-threat; x then obtains a free tempo and the positional advantage. Consequently column 2 is the unique optimal choice.

Final Answer: 2

